[2026-03-16T01:40:46.389Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NKBpPpSHoMmLwfdB6jBoso","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (50KB). Full output saved to: /home/agent/.claude/projects/-repo/1e17ab70-0a33-4d2a-af23-66b102e400e2/tool-results/toolu_01NKBpPpSHoMmLwfdB6jBoso.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n   227→\n   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→\n   230→**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n   231→(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\n   232→API response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\n   233→In observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n   234→\n   235→Use Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\n   236→values or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\n   237→Specifically:\n   238→- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n   239→  output is always sufficient.\n   240→- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n   241→- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n   242→- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n   243→- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n   244→- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n   245→\n   246→In observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\n   247→were unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\n   248→failures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\n   249→responses without visual debugging.\n   250→\n   251→## Debugging\n   252→\n   253→When tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\n   254→mandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n   255→\n   256→0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n...\n</persisted-output>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e17ab70-0a33-4d2a-af23-66b102e400e2","uuid":"49a441fa-edb4-4fe8-b194-b0377c42dac7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"\n## Replay Decision Heuristic\n\n**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\nAPI response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\nIn observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n\nUse Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\nvalues or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\nSpecifically:\n- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n  output is always sufficient.\n- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n\nIn observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\nwere unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\nfailures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\nresponses without visual debugging.\n\n## Debugging\n\nWhen tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\nmandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n\n0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   making any changes.\n6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   on what you found.\n8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n\n### Bug Writeup Template\n\nFor each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n\n```\n# Bug: <test name>\n\n## Step 1: Evidence\n\nEvidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\nresponses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\nno evidence.>\n\nEvidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\nselectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n\n## Step 2: Determination\n\nWhich is broken: <APP or TEST>\n\n## Step 3: Root Cause\n\n<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\nfunction(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n```\n\nDo NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\nDo NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\nDo NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\nThe Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n\nWhen testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n\n## Test Isolation Mandates\n\nThese rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\nfailures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n\n1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   failures. The pattern is:\n   ```typescript\n   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   });\n   ```\n\n2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n\n3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n\n4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n\n5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n\n6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n\n7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n\n8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n\n   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n\n9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n\n10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n\n11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n\n12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n\n12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n\n13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n\n14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   overcounting.\n\n14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n\n15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n\n16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n    duplication.\n\n17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n    was overridden by a later worker.\n\n18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n\n19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n\n## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n\nBefore committing a new spec file, verify:\n- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n\n## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n\nWhen running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\nmust have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\ncauses cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\nstate. Isolation strategies:\n\n1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   re-seed) between each spec file.\n3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   of batch contamination.\n4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs\n   from API responses rather than hardcoding UUIDs. Hardcoded UUIDs that exist in one database\n   state may not exist in the deployed database, causing seed-data-mismatch failures. Always\n   query the API for the list of entities and select by name or attribute, not by UUID.\n6. **JourneyQA must default to deployed URL**: JourneyQA tests should always target the\n   deployed URL, not the local dev server. Local dev server tests consistently fail with\n   Replay's Chromium browser due to browser/server incompatibility. Avoid the local server\n   entirely for JourneyQA tasks.\n\n## React StrictMode and Double-Render\n\nReact StrictMode (enabled by default in development) intentionally double-invokes render\nfunctions, effects, and callbacks to help detect side effects. This causes:\n\n- **Double API calls**: Effects that fetch data will fire twice. Tests that count exact API\n  calls (e.g., expecting exactly 1 fetch) will see 2. Use `waitFor` patterns that assert on\n  DOM state rather than counting network requests.\n- **Race conditions**: Two concurrent fetches for the same data can cause state flicker or\n  stale data display. Components should handle this with cleanup functions in useEffect\n  (abort controllers) or by ignoring stale responses.\n- **Test flakiness**: If tests assert on intermediate render states, StrictMode's extra\n  render cycle can cause intermittent failures. Always wait for the final stable state\n  using `expect(...).toBeVisible()` or `expect(...).toPass()` rather than asserting\n  immediately after navigation.\n\nWhen writing tests, assume double-renders will occur and avoid assertions that depend on\nexact render or fetch counts. Use DOM-based assertions (`toBeVisible`, `toHaveText`) that\nnaturally wait for the final state.\n\n## useEffect Race Conditions\n\nA common pattern in app bugs (observed in multiple test sessions) is `useEffect` hooks that\noverwrite user input with stale API data. This occurs when:\n\n1. User starts editing a form field (local state changes)\n2. An API response arrives (from a fetch triggered by a prior render or StrictMode double-mount)\n3. The `useEffect` that processes the API response overwrites local state, discarding user input\n\n**The fix pattern**: Add an \"editing guard\" that prevents API data from overwriting local state\nwhile the user is actively editing. Two common implementations:\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern 1: useRef editing flag\nconst isEditingRef = useRef(false);\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!isEditingRef.current && apiData) {\n    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n  }\n}, [apiData]);\n\n// Set isEditingRef.current = true in onChange, false in onBlur/onSave\n```\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern 2: pendingSavesRef to track in-flight saves\nconst pendingSavesRef = useRef(0);\n\nconst handleSave = async () => {\n  pendingSavesRef.current++;\n  await saveToApi(value);\n  pendingSavesRef.current--;\n};\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (pendingSavesRef.current === 0 && apiData) {\n    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n  }\n}, [apiData]);\n```\n\nWhen debugging tests where user input appears to be \"lost\" or fields revert to old values,\ncheck for unguarded `useEffect` hooks that sync API data to local state. This pattern is\nmost reliably diagnosed using Replay recordings to observe the exact sequence of state updates.\n\n## Test Counting\n\nDo NOT use `npx playwright test --list` to count or list tests — it is unreliable and\nfrequently fails or produces unhelpful output (~20% success rate). Instead, use:\n\n```bash\ngrep -c \"test('\" <file>\n```\n\nThis reliably counts the number of tests in a spec file for verifying the per-file test limit.\n\n## Test Command Reliability\n\n`npm run test` has a ~59% clean-pass rate (41% of runs encounter at least one failure).\nTest failures during development are expected — they are part of the test-fix-retest cycle.\nDo not treat a first-run failure as a sign of a fundamental problem. Follow the debugging\nprocess in the section below, and respect the 3-retry limit before changing approach.\n\n## Directives\n\n- Do NOT manually start `netlify dev` for testing. The test script manages the dev server\n  automatically. Only use manual `netlify dev` for one-off curl checks.\n\n- If you do manually start `netlify dev` for curl testing, you MUST pass `--offline` and\n  `--functions ./netlify/functions`. The `--offline` flag is required in this container\n  environment to avoid authentication failures. The `--functions` flag avoids 404 errors on\n  function endpoints (the `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions directory to\n  resolve incorrectly without it). Example:\n  `npx netlify dev --offline --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n\n- The Playwright `webServer` command should also include `--functions ./netlify/functions` when\n  using Netlify Functions, to ensure function endpoints resolve correctly during test runs.\n  Example: `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n\n- After making code changes to Netlify Functions or frontend code during a testing session, ALWAYS\n  restart the dev server before re-running tests. The dev server may cache old function bundles.\n  Kill background processes with `pkill -f \"netlify\"` and `pkill -f \"vite\"` before restarting.\n\n- When a checkDirectives finding suggests removing test code (e.g., \"redundant\" cleanup,\n  \"unnecessary\" helpers), always run the affected tests *before* committing the removal to\n  confirm the change is safe. Cleanup code that looks redundant may be essential for test\n  isolation.\n\n- **Require `data-testid` for all assertable elements.** Tests MUST NOT rely on CSS selectors\n  tied to colors, styles, or computed attributes (e.g., `path[stroke=\"#4d7c5e\"]`,\n  `[style*=\"background: red\"]`). Always use `data-testid` attributes for test selectors.\n  This prevents self-inflicted failures when refactoring tasks (e.g., checkDirectives)\n  replace hardcoded colors with CSS variables.\n\n- **Selector audit step for refactoring tasks.** When checkDirectives removes hardcoded\n  colors or replaces them with CSS variables, the task MUST grep test files for selectors\n  that depend on the affected values before committing (e.g.,\n  `grep -r \"stroke=\\|fill=\\|background:\" tests/`). Update any matching test selectors to\n  use `data-testid` in the same changeset.\n\n- **Use content-based locators over positional.** Tests should use `filter({ hasText })` or\n  `getByText()` instead of `nth()` for dropdown/list assertions to avoid ordering-dependent\n  failures. API response ordering is not guaranteed to match creation order.\n\n- **Use exact text matching for option selection.** When selecting dropdown options or matching\n  text that contains common words (e.g., \"Monthly\", \"Active\"), always use exact matching to\n  avoid strict-mode violations from substring collisions. Use `{ exact: true }` or regex\n  anchors: `getByText('Monthly', { exact: true })` or `hasText: /^Monthly$/`. Without exact\n  matching, \"Monthly\" matches both \"Monthly\" and \"Bi-Monthly\", causing Playwright strict-mode\n  errors.\n\n- **Avoid `.or()` locator patterns.** Playwright's `.or()` combinator creates a locator matching\n  elements from either branch. When both branches resolve to elements on the page, the combined\n  locator matches multiple elements and triggers strict-mode violations. Use a single specific\n  locator (`getByTestId` or `getByRole` with exact matching) instead of `.or()` fallback chains.\n\n- All browsers must run headless. Never use Xvfb, never set `DISPLAY`, never use the `replayio record`\n  CLI (it launches a headed browser). Use `@replayio/playwright` for recordings.\n\n- Do NOT wrap test commands with `timeout` or `nohup`. Neither works reliably in this\n  environment — `timeout` exits with signal errors (exit code 124/137) and `nohup` fails\n  to background processes correctly. Let the test script manage its own lifecycle.\n\n- Tests run serially with `--workers 1`. The test script resets the database between each test.\n\n- Tests MUST NOT run against the production database or the main Neon branch. The test script\n  creates ephemeral Neon branches for test runs and deletes them afterwards.\n\n- NEVER stop, cancel, or skip a Replay recording upload that is in progress. The upload is a\n  prerequisite for Replay MCP analysis, which is mandatory for debugging failures. If the upload\n  is taking a long time, WAIT for it to finish. Do NOT proceed with fixes based on \"enough\n  information\" — you must complete the full upload → MCP analysis → fix cycle. Stopping an\n  upload to \"save time\" violates the debugging process and leads to guesswork-based fixes.\n\n- Always run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` (from the app directory), never by calling\n  `npx playwright test` directly. The test script includes essential pre-test setup (branch\n  creation, schema init, seeding, stale cleanup) that is skipped when running Playwright directly.\n\n## Tips\n\n- When a test times out inside a `.toPass()` block, use `mcp__replay__PlaywrightSteps` first. It\n  shows the exact sequence of Playwright actions with timestamps and return values. Look for a\n  step that is stuck auto-waiting for an element — this reveals nested-wait deadlocks where the\n  DOM changed mid-iteration and an inner `textContent()` or `count()` call is waiting on an\n  element that was removed. The fix is always in the test: replace the iteration loop with a\n  single atomic Playwright assertion (see the nested-wait directive in writeTests.md).\n- When debugging history/timeline tests, check for duplicate entries caused by React re-renders triggering multiple API calls, and check for missing entries from mutation handlers that skip history writes.\n- When many detail-page tests fail with \"expected count > 0, received 0\", the root cause is almost\n  always that the database has no seed data OR the API functions are not working. Check both before\n  attempting to fix individual tests.\n- When filter/search tests return wrong data (e.g., filtering by \"proposal\" returns \"Qualification\"),\n  the root cause may be that the SQL query is silently broken. Verify the API returns correctly\n  filtered results by curling the endpoint directly with filter parameters.\n- Tests that pass individually but fail in the full suite usually indicate data contamination between\n  tests. The test script resets the database between each test, so contamination should not occur.\n  If it does, check that the reset is working correctly.\n- When test IDs differ between spec files (cross-cutting vs page-specific), decide on ONE canonical\n  set of IDs in the component and update whichever test file has fewer references.\n- When testing unauthenticated scenarios with `browser.newContext()`, always pass\n  `storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] }` to ensure a truly empty context. Without this,\n  Supabase or other auth libraries may detect cached sessions from previous test runs, causing\n  \"unauthenticated\" tests to appear authenticated.\n- If many tests suddenly fail with `net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED` or `ERR_CONNECTION_RESET` partway\n  through a test run, the dev server crashed mid-suite. Do NOT debug individual test failures —\n  they are all caused by the same server crash. Re-run the full suite. If the crash recurs, check\n  for memory pressure or port conflicts from zombie processes.\n- Zombie processes (`<defunct>`) from previous `vite` or `netlify` runs are harmless and cannot be\n  killed (they are orphaned child processes waiting for PID 1 to reap them). Ignore them. The\n  Playwright `webServer` config starts fresh processes on different ports and is not affected.\n- **Kill stale processes before every test run.** Port conflicts from stale `netlify dev`\n  processes are a significant source of infrastructure failures (20 events affecting 225 test\n  runs in one observed session). Always run the pre-flight cleanup (`pkill -f \"netlify|vite\"`,\n  `fuser -k 8888/tcp`) before each test run, not just the first run in a session.\n- Commit incrementally during debugging sessions. If you improve test results (e.g., 81→207 passing)\n  or fix a meaningful issue, commit those changes immediately even if other failures remain. An\n  iteration that produces zero commits despite significant work (skill updates, code fixes,\n  debugging progress) is wasted effort because the next iteration starts from scratch.\n- **Deployment tests (`tests/deployment.spec.ts`) only work after a deploy** and require a live\n  production URL. Do NOT run deployment tests as part of regular FixTests workflows — they will\n  return 0 tests or fail when run locally without a deployed environment. Only run them after\n  `npm run deploy` has completed successfully.\n- Before running deployment tests, verify that required environment variables (DATABASE_URL,\n  etc.) are set on the deployment target (e.g., Netlify). Missing env vars cause infrastructure\n  failures that waste a full test cycle. Use `netlify env:list` to check.\n- Before running deployment tests, also verify that all Netlify Functions are actually deployed\n  by curling at least one API endpoint. Functions may silently fail to deploy if the `--functions`\n  flag was missing or if there were build errors. A quick `curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\"\n  https://<site-url>/api/<function>` returning 200 confirms functions are live.\n- Before running tests, verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set in the environment. The test script\n  requires it for creating ephemeral Neon branches.\n- Running the full test suite at once (e.g., `npx playwright test` with no file argument) can\n  OOM or crash with `ERR_STRING_TOO_LONG` on large suites. Always run tests one spec file at a\n  time using `npm run test tests/<file>.spec.ts` (which uses the test script that manages\n  database branches). When verifying broad changes, pick the most relevant 2-3 spec files.\n- The database is reset between each test, so tests do not need to worry about data from\n  previous tests. Each test starts with a fresh seed.\n- Seed scripts must always call `truncateAllTables()` (or equivalent) before inserting data\n  to ensure idempotency. Without truncation, re-running seeds causes duplicate key errors.\n  Every test file's `beforeAll`/`beforeEach` should TRUNCATE relevant tables before inserting\n  seed data, not just delete known IDs. Hardcoded ID deletion breaks when Neon branches\n  inherit data from parent branches. **Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()`\n  alone** — Neon ephemeral branches inherit parent data, so insert-only seeding produces\n  duplicates.\n- The Vite dev server cold start can cause the first test in a suite to timeout. Consider adding\n  a warm-up navigation in `beforeAll` or increasing the first test's timeout to account for\n  cold start latency.\n- Avoid hardcoding expected values in assertions. Tests that hardcode specific counts (e.g.,\n  `expect 1 remaining task`) or specific names (e.g., `\"David Lee\"`) break when test data\n  changes. Use relative assertions or query actual seed data to derive expected values.\n  Tests that verify \"a new item was added\" must count items before and after the action,\n  not assert a hardcoded total.\n- **Wait for data before counting rows.** Any test that captures an initial count of list items\n  (for add/delete assertions) MUST first wait for the first row to be visible:\n  ```ts\n  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').first()).toBeVisible();\n  const initialCount = await page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').count();\n  ```\n  Without this wait, async data loading may not have completed, returning 0 and causing\n  off-by-one assertion failures. This is the single highest-impact testing pattern — it\n  prevents ~38% of observed test failures.\n- **Use edge coordinates for backdrop click tests.** Tests for modal dismissal via backdrop/overlay\n  click should always use edge coordinates (e.g., `{ x: 10, y: 10 }`) rather than clicking the\n  center of the overlay, as modals often occupy the center and intercept the click.\n- **Validate seed data counts before asserting.** Tests that assert specific counts from seed data\n  should reference `seed-db.ts` directly or query the actual count rather than hardcoding expected\n  values. Seed data mismatches account for ~42% of observed test failures.\n- Validate that seed data exists before asserting on it. If a test expects a specific assignee\n  name or record count, verify the data is present first. This catches seed data mismatches\n  early instead of producing confusing assertion failures.\n- Use unique `data-testid` values across the page. When a page and a modal both contain similar\n  elements (e.g., a technician select), prefix testids with the component context to avoid\n  strict-mode violations (e.g., `modal-technician-select` vs `detail-technician-select`).\n- Before writing tests that rely on `data-testid` attributes, verify those attributes exist in\n  the component source. Missing `data-testid` attributes cause test failures that are easy to\n  prevent with a quick source check.\n- When seed data uses `NUMERIC` or `DECIMAL` database columns, expect formatted output in\n  assertions (e.g., `\"500\"` not `\"500.00\"`). These column types return decimal strings from\n  PostgreSQL, so the app must format them before display. If tests fail on numeric values,\n  check the column type and app formatting layer before modifying test expectations.\n- When navigation timeouts persist across retries for a specific app (e.g., all supplier-details\n  pages timing out), this is likely a Replay browser resource issue rather than an app bug.\n  Adding a retry-with-delay (e.g., 5s between retries) can help distinguish transient\n  infrastructure issues from persistent ones. If retries still fail, skip those tests and\n  note the infrastructure issue.\n- Seed data that includes time values should use 24-hour format (e.g., `\"14:30\"` not\n  `\"2:30 PM\"`) to avoid text-based sorting issues in SQL. When PostgreSQL sorts TIME or\n  text-stored time values, 24-hour format produces correct chronological order while AM/PM\n  format does not (e.g., `\"10:00 AM\"` sorts after `\"1:00 PM\"` alphabetically).\n- Backend API functions should return date fields in a consistent format — either always\n  ISO timestamps (`2026-01-15T00:00:00.000Z`) or always `YYYY-MM-DD` strings, not a mix\n  of both. Mixed formats cause frontend parsing issues when components expect one format\n  but receive the other.\n- **Format currency values with `.toFixed(2)`.** All currency display components must format\n  values with 2 decimal places (e.g., `175.00` not `175`). Multiple test failures arise from\n  assertions expecting formatted currency strings. Apply `.toFixed(2)` or equivalent formatting\n  at the display layer for any monetary amount.\n- **Use `type=\"text\" inputMode=\"decimal\"` for currency inputs from the start.** Avoid\n  `<input type=\"number\">` for currency fields — it strips formatting and causes issues with\n  decimal display. Using `type=\"text\"` with `inputMode=\"decimal\"` provides the numeric\n  keyboard on mobile while allowing full control over formatting. Converting from `type=\"number\"`\n  to `type=\"text\"` mid-stream breaks existing test expectations.\n- **Seed data MUST use relative date offsets from `new Date()`** rather than hardcoded date\n  strings or month names. Tests that assert on date-filtered data (e.g., expecting \"Jan\"\n  entries or \"No upcoming visits\") will fail when run on different days or months. Use\n  expressions like `new Date(Date.now() + 86400000)` for \"tomorrow\" or subtract days for\n  past dates. This prevents failures when tests run on different days than when seeds were\n  written — hardcoded dates caused a cluster of 3 test failures in one observed session.\n- **Verify directories before navigating.** Before using `cd` to navigate to an app directory,\n  verify it exists with `test -d` or `ls`. Directory navigation failures (`cd` to nonexistent\n  paths) are the most common command failure across all worker iterations.\n- Avoid redundant file exploration commands (`ls /repo/apps/`, `find ... -type f`, etc.)\n  across test runs. Once you know the project structure, do not re-discover it in every\n  iteration. Use the Glob and Grep tools instead of shell commands for file operations.\n\n- **Verify seed data covers all test tables.** After writing or modifying seed-database\n  functions, verify that all tables referenced in tests are properly seeded. Missing table\n  seeding (e.g., categories table truncated but never re-seeded) causes tests to time out\n  waiting for data that doesn't exist. Check each `TRUNCATE` call has a corresponding `INSERT`.\n\n- **Validate Netlify function URL routing.** After writing backend functions, verify that\n  `[[redirects]]` in `netlify.toml` includes all `/api/*` patterns and that URL segment\n  parsing uses the correct index. A recurring bug is `segments[N]` off-by-one errors where\n  the function reads `undefined` instead of the resource ID, returning 405. This appeared in\n  3 separate functions in one session.\n\n- **Cross-spec learning**: When a fix pattern is discovered in one spec file (e.g.,\n  wait-before-count, destructive test reordering, formatDate normalization), proactively apply\n  it to all other spec files in the same app before re-running tests. Fixing each file\n  independently wastes re-run cycles on the same known issue.\n- **Validate journey/QA test prerequisites.** Before running journey or QA tests that depend on\n  specific data states (e.g., member with points > 0, active subscriptions), verify the test data\n  meets preconditions. A test that selects a member with 0 points to test redemption will fail at\n  the UI level (disabled button) rather than giving a clear data error. Add precondition checks\n  or use seed data that guarantees the required state.\n- When many tests are pre-existing failures unrelated to the current task, avoid re-verifying\n  them on every run. Use the `git stash` triage approach (see `skills/debugging/README.md`)\n  once per task to confirm, then focus on new failures only.\n- Before writing test assertions on data counts or relationships, verify expectations against\n  actual seed data. 30% of observed failures were seed-data-mismatch — tests assumed wrong\n  counts or relationships that didn't exist in the database.\n- When building forms with custom validation, always add `noValidate` to the `<form>` element\n  to prevent native browser validation from blocking custom logic. Without `noValidate`,\n  `<input type=\"number\">` and `<input type=\"email\">` have built-in validation that fires\n  before the form's `onSubmit` handler, causing custom validation tests to fail silently.\n- Establish a consistent date handling convention: components should always format dates from\n  ISO timestamps (what the API returns), and date inputs should always use `YYYY-MM-DD`\n  format. Mismatches between API response format and component expectations are a recurring\n  source of failures. All date comparisons in frontend code and tests must normalize to\n  `YYYY-MM-DD` format before comparing, using `.split('T')[0]` or equivalent, since Postgres\n  TIMESTAMP/DATE columns may return ISO strings with time components.\n- When filtering by status values like \"Active\"/\"Inactive\", use exact text matching\n  (e.g., `getByRole('option', { name: /^Active$/ })`) to avoid Playwright strict-mode\n  violations from substring collisions (e.g., \"Active\" matching both \"Active\" and \"Inactive\").\n- Tests should never use raw HTML element selectors (`tr`, `td`, `li`) to locate rows or items.\n  Always use `data-testid` attributes instead. Raw element selectors break when the component's\n  HTML structure changes (e.g., switching from `<table>` to `<div>`-based layout), causing\n  timeouts that are hard to diagnose.\n- **Click visible labels for custom checkboxes/toggles.** When testing checkboxes or toggle\n  inputs with custom CSS styling, the native `<input>` may be hidden and overlaid by a styled\n  element (e.g., `<span>`, `<label>`). Playwright's `click()` on the hidden input will fail\n  with \"click intercepted by...\" errors. Always click the visible label or wrapper element\n  instead of the hidden input. Check the component source to identify which element is visually\n  clickable.\n\n- **Document seed data in test specs.** Test spec files should include a comment at the top\n  documenting what seed data exists (e.g., number of pre-existing records, their names/types)\n  so test authors can account for it upfront rather than discovering mismatches at runtime.\n\n- **Use `click()` + `type()` instead of `fill()` for onChange-dependent inputs.** Playwright's\n  `fill()` sets the input value directly and only fires `input` and `change` events at the end,\n  which may not trigger React's synthetic `onChange` handler in all component implementations.\n  If `fill()` doesn't trigger the expected behavior, switch to `click()` on the input followed\n  by `type()` to simulate real keystrokes, which fires `onChange` on each character.\n\n- **Trigger onBlur after fill() for blur-persisted forms.** When testing form components that\n  use `onBlur` for persistence (auto-save on blur), Playwright `fill()` alone won't trigger\n  the save. Always add `.blur()` after `.fill()` calls in these cases:\n  ```ts\n  await page.locator('#field').fill('value');\n  await page.locator('#field').blur();\n  ```\n\n- **Use ESM-compatible __dirname.** If test helpers or server code use `__dirname`, ensure\n  ESM-compatible alternatives are used: `import.meta.dirname` (Node 21.2+) or\n  `path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))`. CommonJS `__dirname` is not available in\n  ESM modules and will crash at runtime.\n\n- **Add useEffect editing guards for editable forms.** Any React form that loads data via\n  useEffect and allows editing must include an `isEditing` state guard that prevents useEffect\n  from overwriting user input during editing. Set `isEditing = true` when the user begins\n  editing, and skip the data-loading useEffect when `isEditing` is true. This pattern was\n  needed across 3 spec files (7 tests) in one session — apply it proactively to all edit\n  forms during initial component development.\n- Ensure tests don't leak state between runs. Data-contamination failures occur when a prior\n  test's API calls complete after the next test has started, polluting the data state. Use\n  `test.describe.serial` for tests that share mutable state, or ensure API calls are fully\n  settled before test completion.\n- **Validate test data against DB CHECK constraints.** Before using enum values in test data\n  (e.g., `visit_type`, `status`), verify them against the database schema's CHECK constraints\n  or enum definitions. Invalid enum values cause constraint violations that surface as cryptic\n  500 errors. This pattern appeared in 2+ spec files in observed sessions.\n- Any test that creates records (orders, customers, etc.) should include a `beforeEach`\n  cleanup helper (e.g., `deleteAllOrders()`) to prevent data accumulation across tests.\n  Add this from the start when writing tests — don't wait for contamination failures.\n- **Reset global settings in beforeEach.** Since settings are typically a single-row table,\n  any spec that tests settings modifications (business name, preferences, etc.) should reset\n  settings to known defaults in a `beforeEach` hook via API call. Otherwise, earlier tests\n  that modify settings will contaminate later tests in the same file.\n- Any test that deletes all records (e.g., empty state tests) must be in a\n  `test.describe.serial` block at the end of the describe. Destructive tests placed earlier\n  corrupt state for subsequent tests in the same file.\n\n- **Run targeted tests after accessibility refactors.** After any HTML structure change\n  (element type changes like `<span>` to `<th>`, nesting changes), run the spec file for\n  that component before committing. `npm run check` does not catch selector breakage — only\n  running the actual tests will reveal it. This applies especially to polishApp accessibility\n  refactors.\n\n- **JourneyQA tests must not assume live data state.** Do not assume records from prior test\n  runs persist in the deployed database. Either (a) create all required seed data\n  programmatically within the test, or (b) query the API at the start of the test to find an\n  existing record rather than hard-coding an ID or name.\n\n- **Prefer `toContainText` for cells with potential sr-only text.** When asserting on table\n  cells, score displays, or other elements that may contain visually-hidden accessibility\n  annotations (e.g., sr-only \"(leader)\" spans), use `toContainText` instead of `toHaveText`.\n  `toHaveText` requires an exact match including hidden text, which breaks when accessibility\n  improvements add sr-only content. This pattern caused a cluster of 9 test failures across\n  multiple spec files in one observed session — all resolved by switching to `toContainText`.\n\n- **Apply race-condition fixes across all slices at once.** When a race-condition fix (e.g.,\n  request-id tracking) is applied to one Redux slice, check whether other slices using the\n  same fetching pattern need the same fix, and apply it proactively. This avoids discovering\n  the same bug independently in each slice across multiple test-fix cycles.\n\n- **Check test suite after cascade-delete fixes.** When fixing a backend DELETE endpoint to\n  add cascade behavior (e.g., deleting dependent records before the parent), immediately check\n  the test suite for any test that relies on the cascaded data existing later in the file.\n  Fix both the backend and the test ordering in the same changeset — otherwise the cascade fix\n  enables a destructive test to wipe data that subsequent tests depend on.\n\n- **Deploy before writing journey tests.** Journey tests that run against a deployed URL\n  require the correct app to be deployed and the production DB seeded. Verify the deployed URL\n  in `playwright.config.ts` before writing selectors. Do not write journey tests against a URL\n  that belongs to a different app.\n","numLines":698,"startLine":227,"totalLines":924}}}
[2026-03-16T01:40:48.753Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V11uK9EjUyueggZNKCUQTq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0137LUU5C2jvhvhceN5dxnXa","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","offset":255},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":694,"cache_read_input_tokens":24322,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":694,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e17ab70-0a33-4d2a-af23-66b102e400e2","uuid":"8b49b4be-c818-4898-b46d-3979249f2565"}
[2026-03-16T01:40:48.997Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0137LUU5C2jvhvhceN5dxnXa","type":"tool_result","content":"   255→\n   256→0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   257→   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   258→   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   259→   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n   260→1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n   261→2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   262→   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   263→   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   264→   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   265→   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   266→   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   267→   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   268→   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   269→   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n   270→3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   271→   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   272→   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   273→   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   274→   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   275→   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n   276→4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n   277→5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   278→   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   279→   making any changes.\n   280→6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   281→   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n   282→7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   283→   on what you found.\n   284→8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   285→   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   286→   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n   287→\n   288→### Bug Writeup Template\n   289→\n   290→For each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n   291→\n   292→```\n   293→# Bug: <test name>\n   294→\n   295→## Step 1: Evidence\n   296→\n   297→Evidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\n   298→responses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\n   299→no evidence.>\n   300→\n   301→Evidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\n   302→selectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n   303→\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n   304→\n   305→## Step 2: Determination\n   306→\n   307→Which is broken: <APP or TEST>\n   308→\n   309→## Step 3: Root Cause\n   310→\n   311→<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\n   312→function(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n   313→```\n   314→\n   315→Do NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\n   316→Do NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\n   317→Do NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\n   318→The Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n   319→\n   320→When testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n   321→\n   322→## Test Isolation Mandates\n   323→\n   324→These rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\n   325→failures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n   326→\n   327→1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   328→   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   329→   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   330→   failures. The pattern is:\n   331→   ```typescript\n   332→   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n   333→     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n   334→     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n   335→     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   336→   });\n   337→   ```\n   338→\n   339→2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   340→   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   341→   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n   342→\n   343→3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   344→   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   345→   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   346→   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   347→   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   348→   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   349→   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   350→   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n   351→\n   352→4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   353→   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   354→   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   355→   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   356→   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n   357→\n   358→5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   359→   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   360→   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n   361→\n   362→6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   363→   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n   364→\n   365→7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   366→   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   367→   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   368→   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   369→   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n   370→\n   371→8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   372→   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   373→   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   374→   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   375→   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   376→   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   377→   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n   378→\n   379→   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   380→   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   381→   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   382→   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   383→   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n   384→\n   385→9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   386→   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   387→   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n   388→\n   389→10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   390→   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   391→   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   392→   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n   393→\n   394→11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n   395→    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n   396→    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n   397→    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n   398→    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n   399→    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n   400→\n   401→12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   402→   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   403→   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   404→   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   405→   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n   406→\n   407→12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   408→   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   409→   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   410→   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n   411→\n   412→13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n   413→    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n   414→    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n   415→    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n   416→\n   417→14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   418→   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   419→   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   420→   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   421→   overcounting.\n   422→\n   423→14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n   424→    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n   425→    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n   426→    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n   427→    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n   428→    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n   429→\n   430→15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n   431→    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n   432→    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n   433→    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n   434→\n   435→16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n   436→    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n   437→    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n   438→    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n   439→    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n   440→    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n   441→    duplication.\n   442→\n   443→17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n   444→    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n   445→    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n   446→    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n   447→    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n   448→    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n   449→    was overridden by a later worker.\n   450→\n   451→18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n   452→    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n   453→    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n   454→    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n   455→    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n   456→    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n   457→\n   458→19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n   459→    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n   460→    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n   461→    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n   462→    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n   463→\n   464→## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n   465→\n   466→Before committing a new spec file, verify:\n   467→- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n   468→- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n   469→- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n   470→- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n   471→- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n   472→\n   473→## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n   474→\n   475→When running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\n   476→must have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\n   477→causes cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\n   478→state. Isolation strategies:\n   479→\n   480→1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n   481→2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   482→   re-seed) between each spec file.\n   483→3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   484→   of batch contamination.\n   485→4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   486→   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   487→   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   488→   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n   489→5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs\n   490→   from API responses rather than hardcoding UUIDs. Hardcoded UUIDs that exist in one database\n   491→   state may not exist in the deployed database, causing seed-data-mismatch failures. Always\n   492→   query the API for the list of entities and select by name or attribute, not by UUID.\n   493→6. **JourneyQA must default to deployed URL**: JourneyQA tests should always target the\n   494→   deployed URL, not the local dev server. Local dev server tests consistently fail with\n   495→   Replay's Chromium browser due to browser/server incompatibility. Avoid the local server\n   496→   entirely for JourneyQA tasks.\n   497→\n   498→## React StrictMode and Double-Render\n   499→\n   500→React StrictMode (enabled by default in development) intentionally double-invokes render\n   501→functions, effects, and callbacks to help detect side effects. This causes:\n   502→\n   503→- **Double API calls**: Effects that fetch data will fire twice. Tests that count exact API\n   504→  calls (e.g., expecting exactly 1 fetch) will see 2. Use `waitFor` patterns that assert on\n   505→  DOM state rather than counting network requests.\n   506→- **Race conditions**: Two concurrent fetches for the same data can cause state flicker or\n   507→  stale data display. Components should handle this with cleanup functions in useEffect\n   508→  (abort controllers) or by ignoring stale responses.\n   509→- **Test flakiness**: If tests assert on intermediate render states, StrictMode's extra\n   510→  render cycle can cause intermittent failures. Always wait for the final stable state\n   511→  using `expect(...).toBeVisible()` or `expect(...).toPass()` rather than asserting\n   512→  immediately after navigation.\n   513→\n   514→When writing tests, assume double-renders will occur and avoid assertions that depend on\n   515→exact render or fetch counts. Use DOM-based assertions (`toBeVisible`, `toHaveText`) that\n   516→naturally wait for the final state.\n   517→\n   518→## useEffect Race Conditions\n   519→\n   520→A common pattern in app bugs (observed in multiple test sessions) is `useEffect` hooks that\n   521→overwrite user input with stale API data. This occurs when:\n   522→\n   523→1. User starts editing a form field (local state changes)\n   524→2. An API response arrives (from a fetch triggered by a prior render or StrictMode double-mount)\n   525→3. The `useEffect` that processes the API response overwrites local state, discarding user input\n   526→\n   527→**The fix pattern**: Add an \"editing guard\" that prevents API data from overwriting local state\n   528→while the user is actively editing. Two common implementations:\n   529→\n   530→```typescript\n   531→// Pattern 1: useRef editing flag\n   532→const isEditingRef = useRef(false);\n   533→\n   534→useEffect(() => {\n   535→  if (!isEditingRef.current && apiData) {\n   536→    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n   537→  }\n   538→}, [apiData]);\n   539→\n   540→// Set isEditingRef.current = true in onChange, false in onBlur/onSave\n   541→```\n   542→\n   543→```typescript\n   544→// Pattern 2: pendingSavesRef to track in-flight saves\n   545→const pendingSavesRef = useRef(0);\n   546→\n   547→const handleSave = async () => {\n   548→  pendingSavesRef.current++;\n   549→  await saveToApi(value);\n   550→  pendingSavesRef.current--;\n   551→};\n   552→\n   553→useEffect(() => {\n   554→  if (pendingSavesRef.current === 0 && apiData) {\n   555→    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n   556→  }\n   557→}, [apiData]);\n   558→```\n   559→\n   560→When debugging tests where user input appears to be \"lost\" or fields revert to old values,\n   561→check for unguarded `useEffect` hooks that sync API data to local state. This pattern is\n   562→most reliably diagnosed using Replay recordings to observe the exact sequence of state updates.\n   563→\n   564→## Test Counting\n   565→\n   566→Do NOT use `npx playwright test --list` to count or list tests — it is unreliable and\n   567→frequently fails or produces unhelpful output (~20% success rate). Instead, use:\n   568→\n   569→```bash\n   570→grep -c \"test('\" <file>\n   571→```\n   572→\n   573→This reliably counts the number of tests in a spec file for verifying the per-file test limit.\n   574→\n   575→## Test Command Reliability\n   576→\n   577→`npm run test` has a ~59% clean-pass rate (41% of runs encounter at least one failure).\n   578→Test failures during development are expected — they are part of the test-fix-retest cycle.\n   579→Do not treat a first-run failure as a sign of a fundamental problem. Follow the debugging\n   580→process in the section below, and respect the 3-retry limit before changing approach.\n   581→\n   582→## Directives\n   583→\n   584→- Do NOT manually start `netlify dev` for testing. The test script manages the dev server\n   585→  automatically. Only use manual `netlify dev` for one-off curl checks.\n   586→\n   587→- If you do manually start `netlify dev` for curl testing, you MUST pass `--offline` and\n   588→  `--functions ./netlify/functions`. The `--offline` flag is required in this container\n   589→  environment to avoid authentication failures. The `--functions` flag avoids 404 errors on\n   590→  function endpoints (the `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions directory to\n   591→  resolve incorrectly without it). Example:\n   592→  `npx netlify dev --offline --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n   593→\n   594→- The Playwright `webServer` command should also include `--functions ./netlify/functions` when\n   595→  using Netlify Functions, to ensure function endpoints resolve correctly during test runs.\n   596→  Example: `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n   597→\n   598→- After making code changes to Netlify Functions or frontend code during a testing session, ALWAYS\n   599→  restart the dev server before re-running tests. The dev server may cache old function bundles.\n   600→  Kill background processes with `pkill -f \"netlify\"` and `pkill -f \"vite\"` before restarting.\n   601→\n   602→- When a checkDirectives finding suggests removing test code (e.g., \"redundant\" cleanup,\n   603→  \"unnecessary\" helpers), always run the affected tests *before* committing the removal to\n   604→  confirm the change is safe. Cleanup code that looks redundant may be essential for test\n   605→  isolation.\n   606→\n   607→- **Require `data-testid` for all assertable elements.** Tests MUST NOT rely on CSS selectors\n   608→  tied to colors, styles, or computed attributes (e.g., `path[stroke=\"#4d7c5e\"]`,\n   609→  `[style*=\"background: red\"]`). Always use `data-testid` attributes for test selectors.\n   610→  This prevents self-inflicted failures when refactoring tasks (e.g., checkDirectives)\n   611→  replace hardcoded colors with CSS variables.\n   612→\n   613→- **Selector audit step for refactoring tasks.** When checkDirectives removes hardcoded\n   614→  colors or replaces them with CSS variables, the task MUST grep test files for selectors\n   615→  that depend on the affected values before committing (e.g.,\n   616→  `grep -r \"stroke=\\|fill=\\|background:\" tests/`). Update any matching test selectors to\n   617→  use `data-testid` in the same changeset.\n   618→\n   619→- **Use content-based locators over positional.** Tests should use `filter({ hasText })` or\n   620→  `getByText()` instead of `nth()` for dropdown/list assertions to avoid ordering-dependent\n   621→  failures. API response ordering is not guaranteed to match creation order.\n   622→\n   623→- **Use exact text matching for option selection.** When selecting dropdown options or matching\n   624→  text that contains common words (e.g., \"Monthly\", \"Active\"), always use exact matching to\n   625→  avoid strict-mode violations from substring collisions. Use `{ exact: true }` or regex\n   626→  anchors: `getByText('Monthly', { exact: true })` or `hasText: /^Monthly$/`. Without exact\n   627→  matching, \"Monthly\" matches both \"Monthly\" and \"Bi-Monthly\", causing Playwright strict-mode\n   628→  errors.\n   629→\n   630→- **Avoid `.or()` locator patterns.** Playwright's `.or()` combinator creates a locator matching\n   631→  elements from either branch. When both branches resolve to elements on the page, the combined\n   632→  locator matches multiple elements and triggers strict-mode violations. Use a single specific\n   633→  locator (`getByTestId` or `getByRole` with exact matching) instead of `.or()` fallback chains.\n   634→\n   635→- All browsers must run headless. Never use Xvfb, never set `DISPLAY`, never use the `replayio record`\n   636→  CLI (it launches a headed browser). Use `@replayio/playwright` for recordings.\n   637→\n   638→- Do NOT wrap test commands with `timeout` or `nohup`. Neither works reliably in this\n   639→  environment — `timeout` exits with signal errors (exit code 124/137) and `nohup` fails\n   640→  to background processes correctly. Let the test script manage its own lifecycle.\n   641→\n   642→- Tests run serially with `--workers 1`. The test script resets the database between each test.\n   643→\n   644→- Tests MUST NOT run against the production database or the main Neon branch. The test script\n   645→  creates ephemeral Neon branches for test runs and deletes them afterwards.\n   646→\n   647→- NEVER stop, cancel, or skip a Replay recording upload that is in progress. The upload is a\n   648→  prerequisite for Replay MCP analysis, which is mandatory for debugging failures. If the upload\n   649→  is taking a long time, WAIT for it to finish. Do NOT proceed with fixes based on \"enough\n   650→  information\" — you must complete the full upload → MCP analysis → fix cycle. Stopping an\n   651→  upload to \"save time\" violates the debugging process and leads to guesswork-based fixes.\n   652→\n   653→- Always run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` (from the app directory), never by calling\n   654→  `npx playwright test` directly. The test script includes essential pre-test setup (branch\n   655→  creation, schema init, seeding, stale cleanup) that is skipped when running Playwright directly.\n   656→\n   657→## Tips\n   658→\n   659→- When a test times out inside a `.toPass()` block, use `mcp__replay__PlaywrightSteps` first. It\n   660→  shows the exact sequence of Playwright actions with timestamps and return values. Look for a\n   661→  step that is stuck auto-waiting for an element — this reveals nested-wait deadlocks where the\n   662→  DOM changed mid-iteration and an inner `textContent()` or `count()` call is waiting on an\n   663→  element that was removed. The fix is always in the test: replace the iteration loop with a\n   664→  single atomic Playwright assertion (see the nested-wait directive in writeTests.md).\n   665→- When debugging history/timeline tests, check for duplicate entries caused by React re-renders triggering multiple API calls, and check for missing entries from mutation handlers that skip history writes.\n   666→- When many detail-page tests fail with \"expected count > 0, received 0\", the root cause is almost\n   667→  always that the database has no seed data OR the API functions are not working. Check both before\n   668→  attempting to fix individual tests.\n   669→- When filter/search tests return wrong data (e.g., filtering by \"proposal\" returns \"Qualification\"),\n   670→  the root cause may be that the SQL query is silently broken. Verify the API returns correctly\n   671→  filtered results by curling the endpoint directly with filter parameters.\n   672→- Tests that pass individually but fail in the full suite usually indicate data contamination between\n   673→  tests. The test script resets the database between each test, so contamination should not occur.\n   674→  If it does, check that the reset is working correctly.\n   675→- When test IDs differ between spec files (cross-cutting vs page-specific), decide on ONE canonical\n   676→  set of IDs in the component and update whichever test file has fewer references.\n   677→- When testing unauthenticated scenarios with `browser.newContext()`, always pass\n   678→  `storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] }` to ensure a truly empty context. Without this,\n   679→  Supabase or other auth libraries may detect cached sessions from previous test runs, causing\n   680→  \"unauthenticated\" tests to appear authenticated.\n   681→- If many tests suddenly fail with `net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED` or `ERR_CONNECTION_RESET` partway\n   682→  through a test run, the dev server crashed mid-suite. Do NOT debug individual test failures —\n   683→  they are all caused by the same server crash. Re-run the full suite. If the crash recurs, check\n   684→  for memory pressure or port conflicts from zombie processes.\n   685→- Zombie processes (`<defunct>`) from previous `vite` or `netlify` runs are harmless and cannot be\n   686→  killed (they are orphaned child processes waiting for PID 1 to reap them). Ignore them. The\n   687→  Playwright `webServer` config starts fresh processes on different ports and is not affected.\n   688→- **Kill stale processes before every test run.** Port conflicts from stale `netlify dev`\n   689→  processes are a significant source of infrastructure failures (20 events affecting 225 test\n   690→  runs in one observed session). Always run the pre-flight cleanup (`pkill -f \"netlify|vite\"`,\n   691→  `fuser -k 8888/tcp`) before each test run, not just the first run in a session.\n   692→- Commit incrementally during debugging sessions. If you improve test results (e.g., 81→207 passing)\n   693→  or fix a meaningful issue, commit those changes immediately even if other failures remain. An\n   694→  iteration that produces zero commits despite significant work (skill updates, code fixes,\n   695→  debugging progress) is wasted effort because the next iteration starts from scratch.\n   696→- **Deployment tests (`tests/deployment.spec.ts`) only work after a deploy** and require a live\n   697→  production URL. Do NOT run deployment tests as part of regular FixTests workflows — they will\n   698→  return 0 tests or fail when run locally without a deployed environment. Only run them after\n   699→  `npm run deploy` has completed successfully.\n   700→- Before running deployment tests, verify that required environment variables (DATABASE_URL,\n   701→  etc.) are set on the deployment target (e.g., Netlify). Missing env vars cause infrastructure\n   702→  failures that waste a full test cycle. Use `netlify env:list` to check.\n   703→- Before running deployment tests, also verify that all Netlify Functions are actually deployed\n   704→  by curling at least one API endpoint. Functions may silently fail to deploy if the `--functions`\n   705→  flag was missing or if there were build errors. A quick `curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\"\n   706→  https://<site-url>/api/<function>` returning 200 confirms functions are live.\n   707→- Before running tests, verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set in the environment. The test script\n   708→  requires it for creating ephemeral Neon branches.\n   709→- Running the full test suite at once (e.g., `npx playwright test` with no file argument) can\n   710→  OOM or crash with `ERR_STRING_TOO_LONG` on large suites. Always run tests one spec file at a\n   711→  time using `npm run test tests/<file>.spec.ts` (which uses the test script that manages\n   712→  database branches). When verifying broad changes, pick the most relevant 2-3 spec files.\n   713→- The database is reset between each test, so tests do not need to worry about data from\n   714→  previous tests. Each test starts with a fresh seed.\n   715→- Seed scripts must always call `truncateAllTables()` (or equivalent) before inserting data\n   716→  to ensure idempotency. Without truncation, re-running seeds causes duplicate key errors.\n   717→  Every test file's `beforeAll`/`beforeEach` should TRUNCATE relevant tables before inserting\n   718→  seed data, not just delete known IDs. Hardcoded ID deletion breaks when Neon branches\n   719→  inherit data from parent branches. **Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()`\n   720→  alone** — Neon ephemeral branches inherit parent data, so insert-only seeding produces\n   721→  duplicates.\n   722→- The Vite dev server cold start can cause the first test in a suite to timeout. Consider adding\n   723→  a warm-up navigation in `beforeAll` or increasing the first test's timeout to account for\n   724→  cold start latency.\n   725→- Avoid hardcoding expected values in assertions. Tests that hardcode specific counts (e.g.,\n   726→  `expect 1 remaining task`) or specific names (e.g., `\"David Lee\"`) break when test data\n   727→  changes. Use relative assertions or query actual seed data to derive expected values.\n   728→  Tests that verify \"a new item was added\" must count items before and after the action,\n   729→  not assert a hardcoded total.\n   730→- **Wait for data before counting rows.** Any test that captures an initial count of list items\n   731→  (for add/delete assertions) MUST first wait for the first row to be visible:\n   732→  ```ts\n   733→  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').first()).toBeVisible();\n   734→  const initialCount = await page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').count();\n   735→  ```\n   736→  Without this wait, async data loading may not have completed, returning 0 and causing\n   737→  off-by-one assertion failures. This is the single highest-impact testing pattern — it\n   738→  prevents ~38% of observed test failures.\n   739→- **Use edge coordinates for backdrop click tests.** Tests for modal dismissal via backdrop/overlay\n   740→  click should always use edge coordinates (e.g., `{ x: 10, y: 10 }`) rather than clicking the\n   741→  center of the overlay, as modals often occupy the center and intercept the click.\n   742→- **Validate seed data counts before asserting.** Tests that assert specific counts from seed data\n   743→  should reference `seed-db.ts` directly or query the actual count rather than hardcoding expected\n   744→  values. Seed data mismatches account for ~42% of observed test failures.\n   745→- Validate that seed data exists before asserting on it. If a test expects a specific assignee\n   746→  name or record count, verify the data is present first. This catches seed data mismatches\n   747→  early instead of producing confusing assertion failures.\n   748→- Use unique `data-testid` values across the page. When a page and a modal both contain similar\n   749→  elements (e.g., a technician select), prefix testids with the component context to avoid\n   750→  strict-mode violations (e.g., `modal-technician-select` vs `detail-technician-select`).\n   751→- Before writing tests that rely on `data-testid` attributes, verify those attributes exist in\n   752→  the component source. Missing `data-testid` attributes cause test failures that are easy to\n   753→  prevent with a quick source check.\n   754→- When seed data uses `NUMERIC` or `DECIMAL` database columns, expect formatted output in\n   755→  assertions (e.g., `\"500\"` not `\"500.00\"`). These column types return decimal strings from\n   756→  PostgreSQL, so the app must format them before display. If tests fail on numeric values,\n   757→  check the column type and app formatting layer before modifying test expectations.\n   758→- When navigation timeouts persist across retries for a specific app (e.g., all supplier-details\n   759→  pages timing out), this is likely a Replay browser resource issue rather than an app bug.\n   760→  Adding a retry-with-delay (e.g., 5s between retries) can help distinguish transient\n   761→  infrastructure issues from persistent ones. If retries still fail, skip those tests and\n   762→  note the infrastructure issue.\n   763→- Seed data that includes time values should use 24-hour format (e.g., `\"14:30\"` not\n   764→  `\"2:30 PM\"`) to avoid text-based sorting issues in SQL. When PostgreSQL sorts TIME or\n   765→  text-stored time values, 24-hour format produces correct chronological order while AM/PM\n   766→  format does not (e.g., `\"10:00 AM\"` sorts after `\"1:00 PM\"` alphabetically).\n   767→- Backend API functions should return date fields in a consistent format — either always\n   768→  ISO timestamps (`2026-01-15T00:00:00.000Z`) or always `YYYY-MM-DD` strings, not a mix\n   769→  of both. Mixed formats cause frontend parsing issues when components expect one format\n   770→  but receive the other.\n   771→- **Format currency values with `.toFixed(2)`.** All currency display components must format\n   772→  values with 2 decimal places (e.g., `175.00` not `175`). Multiple test failures arise from\n   773→  assertions expecting formatted currency strings. Apply `.toFixed(2)` or equivalent formatting\n   774→  at the display layer for any monetary amount.\n   775→- **Use `type=\"text\" inputMode=\"decimal\"` for currency inputs from the start.** Avoid\n   776→  `<input type=\"number\">` for currency fields — it strips formatting and causes issues with\n   777→  decimal display. Using `type=\"text\"` with `inputMode=\"decimal\"` provides the numeric\n   778→  keyboard on mobile while allowing full control over formatting. Converting from `type=\"number\"`\n   779→  to `type=\"text\"` mid-stream breaks existing test expectations.\n   780→- **Seed data MUST use relative date offsets from `new Date()`** rather than hardcoded date\n   781→  strings or month names. Tests that assert on date-filtered data (e.g., expecting \"Jan\"\n   782→  entries or \"No upcoming visits\") will fail when run on different days or months. Use\n   783→  expressions like `new Date(Date.now() + 86400000)` for \"tomorrow\" or subtract days for\n   784→  past dates. This prevents failures when tests run on different days than when seeds were\n   785→  written — hardcoded dates caused a cluster of 3 test failures in one observed session.\n   786→- **Verify directories before navigating.** Before using `cd` to navigate to an app directory,\n   787→  verify it exists with `test -d` or `ls`. Directory navigation failures (`cd` to nonexistent\n   788→  paths) are the most common command failure across all worker iterations.\n   789→- Avoid redundant file exploration commands (`ls /repo/apps/`, `find ... -type f`, etc.)\n   790→  across test runs. Once you know the project structure, do not re-discover it in every\n   791→  iteration. Use the Glob and Grep tools instead of shell commands for file operations.\n   792→\n   793→- **Verify seed data covers all test tables.** After writing or modifying seed-database\n   794→  functions, verify that all tables referenced in tests are properly seeded. Missing table\n   795→  seeding (e.g., categories table truncated but never re-seeded) causes tests to time out\n   796→  waiting for data that doesn't exist. Check each `TRUNCATE` call has a corresponding `INSERT`.\n   797→\n   798→- **Validate Netlify function URL routing.** After writing backend functions, verify that\n   799→  `[[redirects]]` in `netlify.toml` includes all `/api/*` patterns and that URL segment\n   800→  parsing uses the correct index. A recurring bug is `segments[N]` off-by-one errors where\n   801→  the function reads `undefined` instead of the resource ID, returning 405. This appeared in\n   802→  3 separate functions in one session.\n   803→\n   804→- **Cross-spec learning**: When a fix pattern is discovered in one spec file (e.g.,\n   805→  wait-before-count, destructive test reordering, formatDate normalization), proactively apply\n   806→  it to all other spec files in the same app before re-running tests. Fixing each file\n   807→  independently wastes re-run cycles on the same known issue.\n   808→- **Validate journey/QA test prerequisites.** Before running journey or QA tests that depend on\n   809→  specific data states (e.g., member with points > 0, active subscriptions), verify the test data\n   810→  meets preconditions. A test that selects a member with 0 points to test redemption will fail at\n   811→  the UI level (disabled button) rather than giving a clear data error. Add precondition checks\n   812→  or use seed data that guarantees the required state.\n   813→- When many tests are pre-existing failures unrelated to the current task, avoid re-verifying\n   814→  them on every run. Use the `git stash` triage approach (see `skills/debugging/README.md`)\n   815→  once per task to confirm, then focus on new failures only.\n   816→- Before writing test assertions on data counts or relationships, verify expectations against\n   817→  actual seed data. 30% of observed failures were seed-data-mismatch — tests assumed wrong\n   818→  counts or relationships that didn't exist in the database.\n   819→- When building forms with custom validation, always add `noValidate` to the `<form>` element\n   820→  to prevent native browser validation from blocking custom logic. Without `noValidate`,\n   821→  `<input type=\"number\">` and `<input type=\"email\">` have built-in validation that fires\n   822→  before the form's `onSubmit` handler, causing custom validation tests to fail silently.\n   823→- Establish a consistent date handling convention: components should always format dates from\n   824→  ISO timestamps (what the API returns), and date inputs should always use `YYYY-MM-DD`\n   825→  format. Mismatches between API response format and component expectations are a recurring\n   826→  source of failures. All date comparisons in frontend code and tests must normalize to\n   827→  `YYYY-MM-DD` format before comparing, using `.split('T')[0]` or equivalent, since Postgres\n   828→  TIMESTAMP/DATE columns may return ISO strings with time components.\n   829→- When filtering by status values like \"Active\"/\"Inactive\", use exact text matching\n   830→  (e.g., `getByRole('option', { name: /^Active$/ })`) to avoid Playwright strict-mode\n   831→  violations from substring collisions (e.g., \"Active\" matching both \"Active\" and \"Inactive\").\n   832→- Tests should never use raw HTML element selectors (`tr`, `td`, `li`) to locate rows or items.\n   833→  Always use `data-testid` attributes instead. Raw element selectors break when the component's\n   834→  HTML structure changes (e.g., switching from `<table>` to `<div>`-based layout), causing\n   835→  timeouts that are hard to diagnose.\n   836→- **Click visible labels for custom checkboxes/toggles.** When testing checkboxes or toggle\n   837→  inputs with custom CSS styling, the native `<input>` may be hidden and overlaid by a styled\n   838→  element (e.g., `<span>`, `<label>`). Playwright's `click()` on the hidden input will fail\n   839→  with \"click intercepted by...\" errors. Always click the visible label or wrapper element\n   840→  instead of the hidden input. Check the component source to identify which element is visually\n   841→  clickable.\n   842→\n   843→- **Document seed data in test specs.** Test spec files should include a comment at the top\n   844→  documenting what seed data exists (e.g., number of pre-existing records, their names/types)\n   845→  so test authors can account for it upfront rather than discovering mismatches at runtime.\n   846→\n   847→- **Use `click()` + `type()` instead of `fill()` for onChange-dependent inputs.** Playwright's\n   848→  `fill()` sets the input value directly and only fires `input` and `change` events at the end,\n   849→  which may not trigger React's synthetic `onChange` handler in all component implementations.\n   850→  If `fill()` doesn't trigger the expected behavior, switch to `click()` on the input followed\n   851→  by `type()` to simulate real keystrokes, which fires `onChange` on each character.\n   852→\n   853→- **Trigger onBlur after fill() for blur-persisted forms.** When testing form components that\n   854→  use `onBlur` for persistence (auto-save on blur), Playwright `fill()` alone won't trigger\n   855→  the save. Always add `.blur()` after `.fill()` calls in these cases:\n   856→  ```ts\n   857→  await page.locator('#field').fill('value');\n   858→  await page.locator('#field').blur();\n   859→  ```\n   860→\n   861→- **Use ESM-compatible __dirname.** If test helpers or server code use `__dirname`, ensure\n   862→  ESM-compatible alternatives are used: `import.meta.dirname` (Node 21.2+) or\n   863→  `path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))`. CommonJS `__dirname` is not available in\n   864→  ESM modules and will crash at runtime.\n   865→\n   866→- **Add useEffect editing guards for editable forms.** Any React form that loads data via\n   867→  useEffect and allows editing must include an `isEditing` state guard that prevents useEffect\n   868→  from overwriting user input during editing. Set `isEditing = true` when the user begins\n   869→  editing, and skip the data-loading useEffect when `isEditing` is true. This pattern was\n   870→  needed across 3 spec files (7 tests) in one session — apply it proactively to all edit\n   871→  forms during initial component development.\n   872→- Ensure tests don't leak state between runs. Data-contamination failures occur when a prior\n   873→  test's API calls complete after the next test has started, polluting the data state. Use\n   874→  `test.describe.serial` for tests that share mutable state, or ensure API calls are fully\n   875→  settled before test completion.\n   876→- **Validate test data against DB CHECK constraints.** Before using enum values in test data\n   877→  (e.g., `visit_type`, `status`), verify them against the database schema's CHECK constraints\n   878→  or enum definitions. Invalid enum values cause constraint violations that surface as cryptic\n   879→  500 errors. This pattern appeared in 2+ spec files in observed sessions.\n   880→- Any test that creates records (orders, customers, etc.) should include a `beforeEach`\n   881→  cleanup helper (e.g., `deleteAllOrders()`) to prevent data accumulation across tests.\n   882→  Add this from the start when writing tests — don't wait for contamination failures.\n   883→- **Reset global settings in beforeEach.** Since settings are typically a single-row table,\n   884→  any spec that tests settings modifications (business name, preferences, etc.) should reset\n   885→  settings to known defaults in a `beforeEach` hook via API call. Otherwise, earlier tests\n   886→  that modify settings will contaminate later tests in the same file.\n   887→- Any test that deletes all records (e.g., empty state tests) must be in a\n   888→  `test.describe.serial` block at the end of the describe. Destructive tests placed earlier\n   889→  corrupt state for subsequent tests in the same file.\n   890→\n   891→- **Run targeted tests after accessibility refactors.** After any HTML structure change\n   892→  (element type changes like `<span>` to `<th>`, nesting changes), run the spec file for\n   893→  that component before committing. `npm run check` does not catch selector breakage — only\n   894→  running the actual tests will reveal it. This applies especially to polishApp accessibility\n   895→  refactors.\n   896→\n   897→- **JourneyQA tests must not assume live data state.** Do not assume records from prior test\n   898→  runs persist in the deployed database. Either (a) create all required seed data\n   899→  programmatically within the test, or (b) query the API at the start of the test to find an\n   900→  existing record rather than hard-coding an ID or name.\n   901→\n   902→- **Prefer `toContainText` for cells with potential sr-only text.** When asserting on table\n   903→  cells, score displays, or other elements that may contain visually-hidden accessibility\n   904→  annotations (e.g., sr-only \"(leader)\" spans), use `toContainText` instead of `toHaveText`.\n   905→  `toHaveText` requires an exact match including hidden text, which breaks when accessibility\n   906→  improvements add sr-only content. This pattern caused a cluster of 9 test failures across\n   907→  multiple spec files in one observed session — all resolved by switching to `toContainText`.\n   908→\n   909→- **Apply race-condition fixes across all slices at once.** When a race-condition fix (e.g.,\n   910→  request-id tracking) is applied to one Redux slice, check whether other slices using the\n   911→  same fetching pattern need the same fix, and apply it proactively. This avoids discovering\n   912→  the same bug independently in each slice across multiple test-fix cycles.\n   913→\n   914→- **Check test suite after cascade-delete fixes.** When fixing a backend DELETE endpoint to\n   915→  add cascade behavior (e.g., deleting dependent records before the parent), immediately check\n   916→  the test suite for any test that relies on the cascaded data existing later in the file.\n   917→  Fix both the backend and the test ordering in the same changeset — otherwise the cascade fix\n   918→  enables a destructive test to wipe data that subsequent tests depend on.\n   919→\n   920→- **Deploy before writing journey tests.** Journey tests that run against a deployed URL\n   921→  require the correct app to be deployed and the production DB seeded. Verify the deployed URL\n   922→  in `playwright.config.ts` before writing selectors. Do not write journey tests against a URL\n   923→  that belongs to a different app.\n   924→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e17ab70-0a33-4d2a-af23-66b102e400e2","uuid":"29ca4b35-1149-4b7b-b85d-67d7f0743672","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"\n0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   making any changes.\n6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   on what you found.\n8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n\n### Bug Writeup Template\n\nFor each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n\n```\n# Bug: <test name>\n\n## Step 1: Evidence\n\nEvidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\nresponses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\nno evidence.>\n\nEvidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\nselectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n\n## Step 2: Determination\n\nWhich is broken: <APP or TEST>\n\n## Step 3: Root Cause\n\n<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\nfunction(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n```\n\nDo NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\nDo NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\nDo NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\nThe Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n\nWhen testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n\n## Test Isolation Mandates\n\nThese rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\nfailures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n\n1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   failures. The pattern is:\n   ```typescript\n   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   });\n   ```\n\n2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n\n3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n\n4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n\n5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n\n6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n\n7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n\n8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n\n   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n\n9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n\n10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n\n11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n\n12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n\n12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n\n13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n\n14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   overcounting.\n\n14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n\n15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n\n16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n    duplication.\n\n17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n    was overridden by a later worker.\n\n18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n\n19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n\n## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n\nBefore committing a new spec file, verify:\n- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n\n## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n\nWhen running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\nmust have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\ncauses cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\nstate. Isolation strategies:\n\n1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   re-seed) between each spec file.\n3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   of batch contamination.\n4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs\n   from API responses rather than hardcoding UUIDs. Hardcoded UUIDs that exist in one database\n   state may not exist in the deployed database, causing seed-data-mismatch failures. Always\n   query the API for the list of entities and select by name or attribute, not by UUID.\n6. **JourneyQA must default to deployed URL**: JourneyQA tests should always target the\n   deployed URL, not the local dev server. Local dev server tests consistently fail with\n   Replay's Chromium browser due to browser/server incompatibility. Avoid the local server\n   entirely for JourneyQA tasks.\n\n## React StrictMode and Double-Render\n\nReact StrictMode (enabled by default in development) intentionally double-invokes render\nfunctions, effects, and callbacks to help detect side effects. This causes:\n\n- **Double API calls**: Effects that fetch data will fire twice. Tests that count exact API\n  calls (e.g., expecting exactly 1 fetch) will see 2. Use `waitFor` patterns that assert on\n  DOM state rather than counting network requests.\n- **Race conditions**: Two concurrent fetches for the same data can cause state flicker or\n  stale data display. Components should handle this with cleanup functions in useEffect\n  (abort controllers) or by ignoring stale responses.\n- **Test flakiness**: If tests assert on intermediate render states, StrictMode's extra\n  render cycle can cause intermittent failures. Always wait for the final stable state\n  using `expect(...).toBeVisible()` or `expect(...).toPass()` rather than asserting\n  immediately after navigation.\n\nWhen writing tests, assume double-renders will occur and avoid assertions that depend on\nexact render or fetch counts. Use DOM-based assertions (`toBeVisible`, `toHaveText`) that\nnaturally wait for the final state.\n\n## useEffect Race Conditions\n\nA common pattern in app bugs (observed in multiple test sessions) is `useEffect` hooks that\noverwrite user input with stale API data. This occurs when:\n\n1. User starts editing a form field (local state changes)\n2. An API response arrives (from a fetch triggered by a prior render or StrictMode double-mount)\n3. The `useEffect` that processes the API response overwrites local state, discarding user input\n\n**The fix pattern**: Add an \"editing guard\" that prevents API data from overwriting local state\nwhile the user is actively editing. Two common implementations:\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern 1: useRef editing flag\nconst isEditingRef = useRef(false);\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!isEditingRef.current && apiData) {\n    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n  }\n}, [apiData]);\n\n// Set isEditingRef.current = true in onChange, false in onBlur/onSave\n```\n\n```typescript\n// Pattern 2: pendingSavesRef to track in-flight saves\nconst pendingSavesRef = useRef(0);\n\nconst handleSave = async () => {\n  pendingSavesRef.current++;\n  await saveToApi(value);\n  pendingSavesRef.current--;\n};\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (pendingSavesRef.current === 0 && apiData) {\n    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n  }\n}, [apiData]);\n```\n\nWhen debugging tests where user input appears to be \"lost\" or fields revert to old values,\ncheck for unguarded `useEffect` hooks that sync API data to local state. This pattern is\nmost reliably diagnosed using Replay recordings to observe the exact sequence of state updates.\n\n## Test Counting\n\nDo NOT use `npx playwright test --list` to count or list tests — it is unreliable and\nfrequently fails or produces unhelpful output (~20% success rate). Instead, use:\n\n```bash\ngrep -c \"test('\" <file>\n```\n\nThis reliably counts the number of tests in a spec file for verifying the per-file test limit.\n\n## Test Command Reliability\n\n`npm run test` has a ~59% clean-pass rate (41% of runs encounter at least one failure).\nTest failures during development are expected — they are part of the test-fix-retest cycle.\nDo not treat a first-run failure as a sign of a fundamental problem. Follow the debugging\nprocess in the section below, and respect the 3-retry limit before changing approach.\n\n## Directives\n\n- Do NOT manually start `netlify dev` for testing. The test script manages the dev server\n  automatically. Only use manual `netlify dev` for one-off curl checks.\n\n- If you do manually start `netlify dev` for curl testing, you MUST pass `--offline` and\n  `--functions ./netlify/functions`. The `--offline` flag is required in this container\n  environment to avoid authentication failures. The `--functions` flag avoids 404 errors on\n  function endpoints (the `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions directory to\n  resolve incorrectly without it). Example:\n  `npx netlify dev --offline --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n\n- The Playwright `webServer` command should also include `--functions ./netlify/functions` when\n  using Netlify Functions, to ensure function endpoints resolve correctly during test runs.\n  Example: `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n\n- After making code changes to Netlify Functions or frontend code during a testing session, ALWAYS\n  restart the dev server before re-running tests. The dev server may cache old function bundles.\n  Kill background processes with `pkill -f \"netlify\"` and `pkill -f \"vite\"` before restarting.\n\n- When a checkDirectives finding suggests removing test code (e.g., \"redundant\" cleanup,\n  \"unnecessary\" helpers), always run the affected tests *before* committing the removal to\n  confirm the change is safe. Cleanup code that looks redundant may be essential for test\n  isolation.\n\n- **Require `data-testid` for all assertable elements.** Tests MUST NOT rely on CSS selectors\n  tied to colors, styles, or computed attributes (e.g., `path[stroke=\"#4d7c5e\"]`,\n  `[style*=\"background: red\"]`). Always use `data-testid` attributes for test selectors.\n  This prevents self-inflicted failures when refactoring tasks (e.g., checkDirectives)\n  replace hardcoded colors with CSS variables.\n\n- **Selector audit step for refactoring tasks.** When checkDirectives removes hardcoded\n  colors or replaces them with CSS variables, the task MUST grep test files for selectors\n  that depend on the affected values before committing (e.g.,\n  `grep -r \"stroke=\\|fill=\\|background:\" tests/`). Update any matching test selectors to\n  use `data-testid` in the same changeset.\n\n- **Use content-based locators over positional.** Tests should use `filter({ hasText })` or\n  `getByText()` instead of `nth()` for dropdown/list assertions to avoid ordering-dependent\n  failures. API response ordering is not guaranteed to match creation order.\n\n- **Use exact text matching for option selection.** When selecting dropdown options or matching\n  text that contains common words (e.g., \"Monthly\", \"Active\"), always use exact matching to\n  avoid strict-mode violations from substring collisions. Use `{ exact: true }` or regex\n  anchors: `getByText('Monthly', { exact: true })` or `hasText: /^Monthly$/`. Without exact\n  matching, \"Monthly\" matches both \"Monthly\" and \"Bi-Monthly\", causing Playwright strict-mode\n  errors.\n\n- **Avoid `.or()` locator patterns.** Playwright's `.or()` combinator creates a locator matching\n  elements from either branch. When both branches resolve to elements on the page, the combined\n  locator matches multiple elements and triggers strict-mode violations. Use a single specific\n  locator (`getByTestId` or `getByRole` with exact matching) instead of `.or()` fallback chains.\n\n- All browsers must run headless. Never use Xvfb, never set `DISPLAY`, never use the `replayio record`\n  CLI (it launches a headed browser). Use `@replayio/playwright` for recordings.\n\n- Do NOT wrap test commands with `timeout` or `nohup`. Neither works reliably in this\n  environment — `timeout` exits with signal errors (exit code 124/137) and `nohup` fails\n  to background processes correctly. Let the test script manage its own lifecycle.\n\n- Tests run serially with `--workers 1`. The test script resets the database between each test.\n\n- Tests MUST NOT run against the production database or the main Neon branch. The test script\n  creates ephemeral Neon branches for test runs and deletes them afterwards.\n\n- NEVER stop, cancel, or skip a Replay recording upload that is in progress. The upload is a\n  prerequisite for Replay MCP analysis, which is mandatory for debugging failures. If the upload\n  is taking a long time, WAIT for it to finish. Do NOT proceed with fixes based on \"enough\n  information\" — you must complete the full upload → MCP analysis → fix cycle. Stopping an\n  upload to \"save time\" violates the debugging process and leads to guesswork-based fixes.\n\n- Always run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` (from the app directory), never by calling\n  `npx playwright test` directly. The test script includes essential pre-test setup (branch\n  creation, schema init, seeding, stale cleanup) that is skipped when running Playwright directly.\n\n## Tips\n\n- When a test times out inside a `.toPass()` block, use `mcp__replay__PlaywrightSteps` first. It\n  shows the exact sequence of Playwright actions with timestamps and return values. Look for a\n  step that is stuck auto-waiting for an element — this reveals nested-wait deadlocks where the\n  DOM changed mid-iteration and an inner `textContent()` or `count()` call is waiting on an\n  element that was removed. The fix is always in the test: replace the iteration loop with a\n  single atomic Playwright assertion (see the nested-wait directive in writeTests.md).\n- When debugging history/timeline tests, check for duplicate entries caused by React re-renders triggering multiple API calls, and check for missing entries from mutation handlers that skip history writes.\n- When many detail-page tests fail with \"expected count > 0, received 0\", the root cause is almost\n  always that the database has no seed data OR the API functions are not working. Check both before\n  attempting to fix individual tests.\n- When filter/search tests return wrong data (e.g., filtering by \"proposal\" returns \"Qualification\"),\n  the root cause may be that the SQL query is silently broken. Verify the API returns correctly\n  filtered results by curling the endpoint directly with filter parameters.\n- Tests that pass individually but fail in the full suite usually indicate data contamination between\n  tests. The test script resets the database between each test, so contamination should not occur.\n  If it does, check that the reset is working correctly.\n- When test IDs differ between spec files (cross-cutting vs page-specific), decide on ONE canonical\n  set of IDs in the component and update whichever test file has fewer references.\n- When testing unauthenticated scenarios with `browser.newContext()`, always pass\n  `storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] }` to ensure a truly empty context. Without this,\n  Supabase or other auth libraries may detect cached sessions from previous test runs, causing\n  \"unauthenticated\" tests to appear authenticated.\n- If many tests suddenly fail with `net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED` or `ERR_CONNECTION_RESET` partway\n  through a test run, the dev server crashed mid-suite. Do NOT debug individual test failures —\n  they are all caused by the same server crash. Re-run the full suite. If the crash recurs, check\n  for memory pressure or port conflicts from zombie processes.\n- Zombie processes (`<defunct>`) from previous `vite` or `netlify` runs are harmless and cannot be\n  killed (they are orphaned child processes waiting for PID 1 to reap them). Ignore them. The\n  Playwright `webServer` config starts fresh processes on different ports and is not affected.\n- **Kill stale processes before every test run.** Port conflicts from stale `netlify dev`\n  processes are a significant source of infrastructure failures (20 events affecting 225 test\n  runs in one observed session). Always run the pre-flight cleanup (`pkill -f \"netlify|vite\"`,\n  `fuser -k 8888/tcp`) before each test run, not just the first run in a session.\n- Commit incrementally during debugging sessions. If you improve test results (e.g., 81→207 passing)\n  or fix a meaningful issue, commit those changes immediately even if other failures remain. An\n  iteration that produces zero commits despite significant work (skill updates, code fixes,\n  debugging progress) is wasted effort because the next iteration starts from scratch.\n- **Deployment tests (`tests/deployment.spec.ts`) only work after a deploy** and require a live\n  production URL. Do NOT run deployment tests as part of regular FixTests workflows — they will\n  return 0 tests or fail when run locally without a deployed environment. Only run them after\n  `npm run deploy` has completed successfully.\n- Before running deployment tests, verify that required environment variables (DATABASE_URL,\n  etc.) are set on the deployment target (e.g., Netlify). Missing env vars cause infrastructure\n  failures that waste a full test cycle. Use `netlify env:list` to check.\n- Before running deployment tests, also verify that all Netlify Functions are actually deployed\n  by curling at least one API endpoint. Functions may silently fail to deploy if the `--functions`\n  flag was missing or if there were build errors. A quick `curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\"\n  https://<site-url>/api/<function>` returning 200 confirms functions are live.\n- Before running tests, verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set in the environment. The test script\n  requires it for creating ephemeral Neon branches.\n- Running the full test suite at once (e.g., `npx playwright test` with no file argument) can\n  OOM or crash with `ERR_STRING_TOO_LONG` on large suites. Always run tests one spec file at a\n  time using `npm run test tests/<file>.spec.ts` (which uses the test script that manages\n  database branches). When verifying broad changes, pick the most relevant 2-3 spec files.\n- The database is reset between each test, so tests do not need to worry about data from\n  previous tests. Each test starts with a fresh seed.\n- Seed scripts must always call `truncateAllTables()` (or equivalent) before inserting data\n  to ensure idempotency. Without truncation, re-running seeds causes duplicate key errors.\n  Every test file's `beforeAll`/`beforeEach` should TRUNCATE relevant tables before inserting\n  seed data, not just delete known IDs. Hardcoded ID deletion breaks when Neon branches\n  inherit data from parent branches. **Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()`\n  alone** — Neon ephemeral branches inherit parent data, so insert-only seeding produces\n  duplicates.\n- The Vite dev server cold start can cause the first test in a suite to timeout. Consider adding\n  a warm-up navigation in `beforeAll` or increasing the first test's timeout to account for\n  cold start latency.\n- Avoid hardcoding expected values in assertions. Tests that hardcode specific counts (e.g.,\n  `expect 1 remaining task`) or specific names (e.g., `\"David Lee\"`) break when test data\n  changes. Use relative assertions or query actual seed data to derive expected values.\n  Tests that verify \"a new item was added\" must count items before and after the action,\n  not assert a hardcoded total.\n- **Wait for data before counting rows.** Any test that captures an initial count of list items\n  (for add/delete assertions) MUST first wait for the first row to be visible:\n  ```ts\n  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').first()).toBeVisible();\n  const initialCount = await page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').count();\n  ```\n  Without this wait, async data loading may not have completed, returning 0 and causing\n  off-by-one assertion failures. This is the single highest-impact testing pattern — it\n  prevents ~38% of observed test failures.\n- **Use edge coordinates for backdrop click tests.** Tests for modal dismissal via backdrop/overlay\n  click should always use edge coordinates (e.g., `{ x: 10, y: 10 }`) rather than clicking the\n  center of the overlay, as modals often occupy the center and intercept the click.\n- **Validate seed data counts before asserting.** Tests that assert specific counts from seed data\n  should reference `seed-db.ts` directly or query the actual count rather than hardcoding expected\n  values. Seed data mismatches account for ~42% of observed test failures.\n- Validate that seed data exists before asserting on it. If a test expects a specific assignee\n  name or record count, verify the data is present first. This catches seed data mismatches\n  early instead of producing confusing assertion failures.\n- Use unique `data-testid` values across the page. When a page and a modal both contain similar\n  elements (e.g., a technician select), prefix testids with the component context to avoid\n  strict-mode violations (e.g., `modal-technician-select` vs `detail-technician-select`).\n- Before writing tests that rely on `data-testid` attributes, verify those attributes exist in\n  the component source. Missing `data-testid` attributes cause test failures that are easy to\n  prevent with a quick source check.\n- When seed data uses `NUMERIC` or `DECIMAL` database columns, expect formatted output in\n  assertions (e.g., `\"500\"` not `\"500.00\"`). These column types return decimal strings from\n  PostgreSQL, so the app must format them before display. If tests fail on numeric values,\n  check the column type and app formatting layer before modifying test expectations.\n- When navigation timeouts persist across retries for a specific app (e.g., all supplier-details\n  pages timing out), this is likely a Replay browser resource issue rather than an app bug.\n  Adding a retry-with-delay (e.g., 5s between retries) can help distinguish transient\n  infrastructure issues from persistent ones. If retries still fail, skip those tests and\n  note the infrastructure issue.\n- Seed data that includes time values should use 24-hour format (e.g., `\"14:30\"` not\n  `\"2:30 PM\"`) to avoid text-based sorting issues in SQL. When PostgreSQL sorts TIME or\n  text-stored time values, 24-hour format produces correct chronological order while AM/PM\n  format does not (e.g., `\"10:00 AM\"` sorts after `\"1:00 PM\"` alphabetically).\n- Backend API functions should return date fields in a consistent format — either always\n  ISO timestamps (`2026-01-15T00:00:00.000Z`) or always `YYYY-MM-DD` strings, not a mix\n  of both. Mixed formats cause frontend parsing issues when components expect one format\n  but receive the other.\n- **Format currency values with `.toFixed(2)`.** All currency display components must format\n  values with 2 decimal places (e.g., `175.00` not `175`). Multiple test failures arise from\n  assertions expecting formatted currency strings. Apply `.toFixed(2)` or equivalent formatting\n  at the display layer for any monetary amount.\n- **Use `type=\"text\" inputMode=\"decimal\"` for currency inputs from the start.** Avoid\n  `<input type=\"number\">` for currency fields — it strips formatting and causes issues with\n  decimal display. Using `type=\"text\"` with `inputMode=\"decimal\"` provides the numeric\n  keyboard on mobile while allowing full control over formatting. Converting from `type=\"number\"`\n  to `type=\"text\"` mid-stream breaks existing test expectations.\n- **Seed data MUST use relative date offsets from `new Date()`** rather than hardcoded date\n  strings or month names. Tests that assert on date-filtered data (e.g., expecting \"Jan\"\n  entries or \"No upcoming visits\") will fail when run on different days or months. Use\n  expressions like `new Date(Date.now() + 86400000)` for \"tomorrow\" or subtract days for\n  past dates. This prevents failures when tests run on different days than when seeds were\n  written — hardcoded dates caused a cluster of 3 test failures in one observed session.\n- **Verify directories before navigating.** Before using `cd` to navigate to an app directory,\n  verify it exists with `test -d` or `ls`. Directory navigation failures (`cd` to nonexistent\n  paths) are the most common command failure across all worker iterations.\n- Avoid redundant file exploration commands (`ls /repo/apps/`, `find ... -type f`, etc.)\n  across test runs. Once you know the project structure, do not re-discover it in every\n  iteration. Use the Glob and Grep tools instead of shell commands for file operations.\n\n- **Verify seed data covers all test tables.** After writing or modifying seed-database\n  functions, verify that all tables referenced in tests are properly seeded. Missing table\n  seeding (e.g., categories table truncated but never re-seeded) causes tests to time out\n  waiting for data that doesn't exist. Check each `TRUNCATE` call has a corresponding `INSERT`.\n\n- **Validate Netlify function URL routing.** After writing backend functions, verify that\n  `[[redirects]]` in `netlify.toml` includes all `/api/*` patterns and that URL segment\n  parsing uses the correct index. A recurring bug is `segments[N]` off-by-one errors where\n  the function reads `undefined` instead of the resource ID, returning 405. This appeared in\n  3 separate functions in one session.\n\n- **Cross-spec learning**: When a fix pattern is discovered in one spec file (e.g.,\n  wait-before-count, destructive test reordering, formatDate normalization), proactively apply\n  it to all other spec files in the same app before re-running tests. Fixing each file\n  independently wastes re-run cycles on the same known issue.\n- **Validate journey/QA test prerequisites.** Before running journey or QA tests that depend on\n  specific data states (e.g., member with points > 0, active subscriptions), verify the test data\n  meets preconditions. A test that selects a member with 0 points to test redemption will fail at\n  the UI level (disabled button) rather than giving a clear data error. Add precondition checks\n  or use seed data that guarantees the required state.\n- When many tests are pre-existing failures unrelated to the current task, avoid re-verifying\n  them on every run. Use the `git stash` triage approach (see `skills/debugging/README.md`)\n  once per task to confirm, then focus on new failures only.\n- Before writing test assertions on data counts or relationships, verify expectations against\n  actual seed data. 30% of observed failures were seed-data-mismatch — tests assumed wrong\n  counts or relationships that didn't exist in the database.\n- When building forms with custom validation, always add `noValidate` to the `<form>` element\n  to prevent native browser validation from blocking custom logic. Without `noValidate`,\n  `<input type=\"number\">` and `<input type=\"email\">` have built-in validation that fires\n  before the form's `onSubmit` handler, causing custom validation tests to fail silently.\n- Establish a consistent date handling convention: components should always format dates from\n  ISO timestamps (what the API returns), and date inputs should always use `YYYY-MM-DD`\n  format. Mismatches between API response format and component expectations are a recurring\n  source of failures. All date comparisons in frontend code and tests must normalize to\n  `YYYY-MM-DD` format before comparing, using `.split('T')[0]` or equivalent, since Postgres\n  TIMESTAMP/DATE columns may return ISO strings with time components.\n- When filtering by status values like \"Active\"/\"Inactive\", use exact text matching\n  (e.g., `getByRole('option', { name: /^Active$/ })`) to avoid Playwright strict-mode\n  violations from substring collisions (e.g., \"Active\" matching both \"Active\" and \"Inactive\").\n- Tests should never use raw HTML element selectors (`tr`, `td`, `li`) to locate rows or items.\n  Always use `data-testid` attributes instead. Raw element selectors break when the component's\n  HTML structure changes (e.g., switching from `<table>` to `<div>`-based layout), causing\n  timeouts that are hard to diagnose.\n- **Click visible labels for custom checkboxes/toggles.** When testing checkboxes or toggle\n  inputs with custom CSS styling, the native `<input>` may be hidden and overlaid by a styled\n  element (e.g., `<span>`, `<label>`). Playwright's `click()` on the hidden input will fail\n  with \"click intercepted by...\" errors. Always click the visible label or wrapper element\n  instead of the hidden input. Check the component source to identify which element is visually\n  clickable.\n\n- **Document seed data in test specs.** Test spec files should include a comment at the top\n  documenting what seed data exists (e.g., number of pre-existing records, their names/types)\n  so test authors can account for it upfront rather than discovering mismatches at runtime.\n\n- **Use `click()` + `type()` instead of `fill()` for onChange-dependent inputs.** Playwright's\n  `fill()` sets the input value directly and only fires `input` and `change` events at the end,\n  which may not trigger React's synthetic `onChange` handler in all component implementations.\n  If `fill()` doesn't trigger the expected behavior, switch to `click()` on the input followed\n  by `type()` to simulate real keystrokes, which fires `onChange` on each character.\n\n- **Trigger onBlur after fill() for blur-persisted forms.** When testing form components that\n  use `onBlur` for persistence (auto-save on blur), Playwright `fill()` alone won't trigger\n  the save. Always add `.blur()` after `.fill()` calls in these cases:\n  ```ts\n  await page.locator('#field').fill('value');\n  await page.locator('#field').blur();\n  ```\n\n- **Use ESM-compatible __dirname.** If test helpers or server code use `__dirname`, ensure\n  ESM-compatible alternatives are used: `import.meta.dirname` (Node 21.2+) or\n  `path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))`. CommonJS `__dirname` is not available in\n  ESM modules and will crash at runtime.\n\n- **Add useEffect editing guards for editable forms.** Any React form that loads data via\n  useEffect and allows editing must include an `isEditing` state guard that prevents useEffect\n  from overwriting user input during editing. Set `isEditing = true` when the user begins\n  editing, and skip the data-loading useEffect when `isEditing` is true. This pattern was\n  needed across 3 spec files (7 tests) in one session — apply it proactively to all edit\n  forms during initial component development.\n- Ensure tests don't leak state between runs. Data-contamination failures occur when a prior\n  test's API calls complete after the next test has started, polluting the data state. Use\n  `test.describe.serial` for tests that share mutable state, or ensure API calls are fully\n  settled before test completion.\n- **Validate test data against DB CHECK constraints.** Before using enum values in test data\n  (e.g., `visit_type`, `status`), verify them against the database schema's CHECK constraints\n  or enum definitions. Invalid enum values cause constraint violations that surface as cryptic\n  500 errors. This pattern appeared in 2+ spec files in observed sessions.\n- Any test that creates records (orders, customers, etc.) should include a `beforeEach`\n  cleanup helper (e.g., `deleteAllOrders()`) to prevent data accumulation across tests.\n  Add this from the start when writing tests — don't wait for contamination failures.\n- **Reset global settings in beforeEach.** Since settings are typically a single-row table,\n  any spec that tests settings modifications (business name, preferences, etc.) should reset\n  settings to known defaults in a `beforeEach` hook via API call. Otherwise, earlier tests\n  that modify settings will contaminate later tests in the same file.\n- Any test that deletes all records (e.g., empty state tests) must be in a\n  `test.describe.serial` block at the end of the describe. Destructive tests placed earlier\n  corrupt state for subsequent tests in the same file.\n\n- **Run targeted tests after accessibility refactors.** After any HTML structure change\n  (element type changes like `<span>` to `<th>`, nesting changes), run the spec file for\n  that component before committing. `npm run check` does not catch selector breakage — only\n  running the actual tests will reveal it. This applies especially to polishApp accessibility\n  refactors.\n\n- **JourneyQA tests must not assume live data state.** Do not assume records from prior test\n  runs persist in the deployed database. Either (a) create all required seed data\n  programmatically within the test, or (b) query the API at the start of the test to find an\n  existing record rather than hard-coding an ID or name.\n\n- **Prefer `toContainText` for cells with potential sr-only text.** When asserting on table\n  cells, score displays, or other elements that may contain visually-hidden accessibility\n  annotations (e.g., sr-only \"(leader)\" spans), use `toContainText` instead of `toHaveText`.\n  `toHaveText` requires an exact match including hidden text, which breaks when accessibility\n  improvements add sr-only content. This pattern caused a cluster of 9 test failures across\n  multiple spec files in one observed session — all resolved by switching to `toContainText`.\n\n- **Apply race-condition fixes across all slices at once.** When a race-condition fix (e.g.,\n  request-id tracking) is applied to one Redux slice, check whether other slices using the\n  same fetching pattern need the same fix, and apply it proactively. This avoids discovering\n  the same bug independently in each slice across multiple test-fix cycles.\n\n- **Check test suite after cascade-delete fixes.** When fixing a backend DELETE endpoint to\n  add cascade behavior (e.g., deleting dependent records before the parent), immediately check\n  the test suite for any test that relies on the cascaded data existing later in the file.\n  Fix both the backend and the test ordering in the same changeset — otherwise the cascade fix\n  enables a destructive test to wipe data that subsequent tests depend on.\n\n- **Deploy before writing journey tests.** Journey tests that run against a deployed URL\n  require the correct app to be deployed and the production DB seeded. Verify the deployed URL\n  in `playwright.config.ts` before writing selectors. Do not write journey tests against a URL\n  that belongs to a different app.\n","numLines":670,"startLine":255,"totalLines":924}}}
