[2026-03-16T01:43:42.379Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012LQTtKEHRNUyqmvUg8re3L","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I found the netlify function that handles the `/api/teams` endpoint. Here's what I found:\n\n## JSON Structure Returned by `/api/teams` Endpoint\n\nThe `teams.ts` function handles multiple endpoints with different responses:\n\n### GET /api/teams (List all teams)\nReturns this JSON structure:\n```json\n{\n  \"teams\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": string,\n      \"name\": string,\n      \"identifier\": string,\n      \"description\": string,\n      \"member_count\": number,\n      \"active_cycle_name\": string | null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### GET /api/teams/{teamId} (Get single team with members)\nReturns this JSON structure:\n```json\n{\n  \"team\": {\n    \"id\": string,\n    \"name\": string,\n    \"identifier\": string,\n    \"description\": string\n  },\n  \"members\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": string,\n      \"name\": string,\n      \"email\": string\n    }\n  ],\n  \"availableMembers\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": string,\n      \"name\": string,\n      \"email\": string\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### POST /api/teams (Create team)\nReturns:\n```json\n{\n  \"team\": {\n    \"id\": string,\n    \"name\": string,\n    \"identifier\": string,\n    \"description\": string,\n    \"member_count\": 0,\n    \"active_cycle_name\": null\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### PUT /api/teams/{teamId} (Update team)\nReturns:\n```json\n{\n  \"team\": {\n    \"id\": string,\n    \"name\": string,\n    \"identifier\": string,\n    \"description\": string\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### DELETE /api/teams/{teamId}\nReturns:\n```json\n{\n  \"success\": true\n}\n```\n\n## Related Endpoints\nYes, there are other team-related endpoints in the `/netlify/functions/` directory:\n- `/repo/apps/LinearClone/netlify/functions/team-issues.ts` - handles team-specific issues\n- `/repo/apps/LinearClone/netlify/functions/team-members.ts` - handles team member management\n\nThe main `teams.ts` endpoint at `/api/teams` is the primary function for CRUD operations on teams."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a81e04102b85aca8d (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)\n<usage>total_tokens: 21316\ntool_uses: 2\nduration_ms: 6593</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e17ab70-0a33-4d2a-af23-66b102e400e2","uuid":"d7a3b91f-7ef0-4904-8fb8-56b31c987f80","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In /repo/apps/LinearClone, find the netlify function that handles the `/api/teams` endpoint. Read its source code and tell me exactly what JSON structure it returns. Also check if there's a different endpoint for getting teams. Look in netlify/functions/ directory.","agentId":"a81e04102b85aca8d","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I found the netlify function that handles the `/api/teams` endpoint. Here's what I found:\n\n## JSON Structure Returned by `/api/teams` Endpoint\n\nThe `teams.ts` function handles multiple endpoints with different responses:\n\n### GET /api/teams (List all teams)\nReturns this JSON structure:\n```json\n{\n  \"teams\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": string,\n      \"name\": string,\n      \"identifier\": string,\n      \"description\": string,\n      \"member_count\": number,\n      \"active_cycle_name\": string | null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### GET /api/teams/{teamId} (Get single team with members)\nReturns this JSON structure:\n```json\n{\n  \"team\": {\n    \"id\": string,\n    \"name\": string,\n    \"identifier\": string,\n    \"description\": string\n  },\n  \"members\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": string,\n      \"name\": string,\n      \"email\": string\n    }\n  ],\n  \"availableMembers\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": string,\n      \"name\": string,\n      \"email\": string\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### POST /api/teams (Create team)\nReturns:\n```json\n{\n  \"team\": {\n    \"id\": string,\n    \"name\": string,\n    \"identifier\": string,\n    \"description\": string,\n    \"member_count\": 0,\n    \"active_cycle_name\": null\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### PUT /api/teams/{teamId} (Update team)\nReturns:\n```json\n{\n  \"team\": {\n    \"id\": string,\n    \"name\": string,\n    \"identifier\": string,\n    \"description\": string\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### DELETE /api/teams/{teamId}\nReturns:\n```json\n{\n  \"success\": true\n}\n```\n\n## Related Endpoints\nYes, there are other team-related endpoints in the `/netlify/functions/` directory:\n- `/repo/apps/LinearClone/netlify/functions/team-issues.ts` - handles team-specific issues\n- `/repo/apps/LinearClone/netlify/functions/team-members.ts` - handles team member management\n\nThe main `teams.ts` endpoint at `/api/teams` is the primary function for CRUD operations on teams."}],"totalDurationMs":6593,"totalTokens":21316,"totalToolUseCount":2,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3109,"cache_read_input_tokens":17635,"output_tokens":568,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3109},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}}}
[2026-03-16T01:44:11.669Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01U8T3psK67pxZPP6Bv2BXjh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the API returns `{ teams: [...] }` for GET /api/teams. The test's `getTeams` function does:\n\n```typescript\nasync function getTeams(baseURL: string, token: string) {\n  const response = await fetch(`${baseURL}/api/teams`, {\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },\n  });\n  const data = await response.json();\n  return data.teams;\n}\n```\n\nThis should work correctly. The issue is that `teams` is `undefined`. This could mean:\n1. The API is returning an error (not 200), so `data` doesn't have a `teams` property\n\n\n2. The login token is invalid for the specific test\n\nBut wait—the first four tests pass while later ones fail, which suggests a database reset between tests is leaving the server in an inconsistent state or the database needs time to recover. Let me look more carefully at the error logs to understand what's actually happening.\n\nThe first four tests at lines 84, 99, 112, and 125 all pass, but starting from test 5 at line 151, things break differently. Test 5 fails at line 187 with a project dropdown issue, while tests 6 through 14 all fail at line 32 with what looks like the login token problem. This pattern suggests the initial tests work fine, but something degrades after that point.\n\nI'm thinking the issue might be related to how the server handles repeated requests during the test run. The Node.js `fetch` calls in the helper function could be getting overwhelmed or timing out as tests pile up, especially if the database is being reset between each test and the server isn't keeping up with the load. Looking at the test output more carefully, all 14 tests ran with 4 passing and 10 failing, so it's not a parallelization issue. The real problem might be that these `fetch` calls in the Node.js helpers are hitting the server during database