ations` was rejected because the auth token wasn't properly set in the Redux state when the thunk executed.\n\nLooking at how the thunk accesses the token from state, it should work fine—when Bob navigates to `/inbox`, his token is already loaded from localStorage, so by the time `fetchNotifications` dispatches, the token should be available for the API call.\n\nBut wait, there's something odd here. The sidebar shows \"Inbox 1\" indicating one unread notification, yet the page itself displays \"You're all caught up!\" with an empty list. That shouldn't happen unless there's a race condition—maybe `fetchUnreadCount` (which runs in the sidebar) set the count to 1, but then `fetchNotifications` returned an empty array. Let me check what the extraReducers in the notifications slice are actually doing...\n\nLooking at how these reducers work, `fetchUnreadCount` sets the count directly from the API response, while `fetchNotifications` both updates the items list and recalculates the unread count based on filtering. So if `fetchNotifications` completes after `fetchUnreadCount`, it would overwrite the count to 0. The fact that we're seeing 1 suggests `fetchNotifications` either hasn't finished yet or failed entirely—probably because the API call to get notifications returned an error.\n\nGiven the Neon database connection issues we've seen before, the notifications endpoint is likely timing out or failing silently while the count endpoint succeeds. For the test, I should either add a page reload to retry loading notifications or use `toPass()` to wait for the assertion to succeed. Let me also check what the sidebar is showing for the notification count to confirm this theory.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":624,"cache_read_input_tokens":149194,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":624,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"