[2026-03-16T02:02:04.422Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CnbztDBF7uRqq7oCXYwf7d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see:\n\n1. The notification message format is: `${creatorName} assigned you to ${identifier}: ${title}`\n   Example: \"Alice Johnson assigned you to ENG-9: Review PR 1234\"\n\n2. The test asserts:\n   - `toContainText('Alice Johnson assigned you to')` ✅ matches\n   - `toContainText(uniqueTitle)` ✅ matches\n\n3. But the notification type is `'assigned'`, not `'assignment'`. Let me check the notifications API to see how it filters.\n\nWait, the `notifications.ts` endpoint fetches all non-archived notifications. The type is stored as `'assigned'` but the Notification interface uses `'assignment' | 'update' | 'mention'`. \n\nThere's a type mismatch here—the database stores `'assigned'` while the interface expects `'assignment'`, though this probably doesn't cause runtime errors since it's just a string value. The real problem is likely that the inbox page shows \"You're all caught up!\" when notifications should be visible, which points to a timing or connection issue rather than the type discrepancy.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2409,"cache_read_input_tokens":149818,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2409,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e17ab70-0a33-4d2a-af23-66b102e400e2","uuid":"4f12c272-c7e6-43d7-9dfc-bfacdcfc1122"}
